                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "b70fa861-31ae-3149-8bdb-119dd9ce6c45",
                        "entry_id": "e269c609-617a-317a-ab7a-af0a1696d70b",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A peplos, an Ancient Greek garment formed of a tubular piece of cloth folded back upon itself halfway down so that the top of the tube is worn around the waist, and the bottom covers the legs down to the ankles; the open top is then worn over the shoulders, and draped, in folds, down to the waist. Compare the Roman palla."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "fc356927-01a8-31b9-928a-1dd6b6053cd8",
                        "entry_id": "e269c609-617a-317a-ab7a-af0a1696d70b",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A kind of women's outer garment in ancient Greece; a veil."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "0b93de36-7210-4ecc-993a-9d215710427d",
                                "sense_id": "fc356927-01a8-31b9-928a-1dd6b6053cd8",
                                "text": "And this was Neith, or Athena, that God thus described, \"I am all that was, is, and shall be, and my peplum or veil, no mortal could ever uncover.\" […] Peplum is properly a womanish pall or veil, embroidered all over and consecrated to Minerva."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b590366e-db62-4658-bab6-da46e3097ed1",
                                "sense_id": "fc356927-01a8-31b9-928a-1dd6b6053cd8",
                                "text": "1838: The North American Review, volume XLVII\nThe outer garment was called the peplum, and was used more for occasions of ceremony than for ordinary convenience, as it was very long and ample, and, from the manner of putting it on, must have been inconvenient to the wearer. It was sometimes wound double round the body, […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5285da26-1b12-4b46-8ecd-9eddf71ee7a5",
                                "sense_id": "fc356927-01a8-31b9-928a-1dd6b6053cd8",
                                "text": "Over this they adopted the Greek peplum, under the name of palla. This garment, however, was exclusively confined to the gentler sex, and was never worn, as was the peplum among the Greeks, by men."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "6b891d6f-0e7b-302a-815d-61518d5f562f",
                        "entry_id": "e269c609-617a-317a-ab7a-af0a1696d70b",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A short overskirt cut in such a way that the sides form points."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "7d21968c-8bc0-378c-8293-2d983349ae1c",
                        "entry_id": "e269c609-617a-317a-ab7a-af0a1696d70b",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A short overskirt attached to the waist of a garment; a basque."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    }
